{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>You shall not offer on it strange incense.</b> Scripture warns that they should not preempt the incense of spices by offering anything on the altar — neither the incense of an individual nor the incense of the public brought as a freewill offering, and certainly not some other kind. Or it warns that they should not offer on the altar incense from other spices, but only from those that Scripture will specify; and if one did so it would be called 'strange' and he would transgress this prohibition. <b>And a burnt-offering.</b> Such as the blood of a bird burnt-offering, which is a small thing. <b>And a meal-offering.</b> Such as the handful of a meal-offering. <b>And a drink-offering.</b> Even a water libation. And the Torah fixed the time of the burning of the incense at the time when the oil is brought near, from what Scripture mentions: 'and Aaron shall burn on it incense of spices; in the morning, in the morning, when he tends the lamps, he shall burn it'; and it is written: 'and when Aaron lights the lamps at twilight, he shall burn it'; and concerning this Solomon said (Proverbs 27): ״שמן וקטורת ישמח לב״ — oil and incense rejoice the heart." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״שמן וקטורת ישמח לב״ — oil, in that the High Priest was anointed with the anointing oil; and incense, in that he would burn it in the innermost sanctuary, and it would rise and ascend. ״ישמח לב״ — all Israel would rejoice and know that their sins were atoned and his deeds accepted. You find that offerings were never praised as highly as the offerings of the princes, because they offered the incense first, as it says (Numbers 7:14–15): ״כף אחת עשרה זהב מלאה קטרת״ — one gold pan of ten shekels, full of incense — and afterward: ״פר אחד בן בקר״ — one young bull. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, praises their offerings individually: 'the offering of so-and-so, the offering of so-and-so.' And likewise you find concerning Ahaz, who annulled the Torah, as it says (Isaiah 8:16): ״צור תעודה חתום תורה בלמודי״ — bind up the testimony, seal the Torah among My disciples — and he served idolatry and multiplied provocation, and the Holy One, blessed be He, forgave him all the other transgressions but did not forgive him for the nullification of the incense, as it is written (II Chronicles 29:7): ״גם סגרו דלתות האולם ויכבו את הנרות וקטרת לא הקטירו ועולה לא העלו בקדש לאלהי ישראל ויהי קצף ה׳ על יהודה וירושלם ויתנם לזעוה״ — they also shut the doors of the porch and put out the lamps and did not burn incense and did not offer burnt-offering in the holy place to the God of Israel, and the wrath of YHWH was upon Judah and Jerusalem and He gave them for terror. How beloved is incense before the Holy One, blessed be He: the Tabernacle was made with all its vessels, and the offerings were slaughtered and arranged on the altar, and the table and the lampstand were set — yet the Shekhinah did not descend until they offered the incense. And likewise we find that Solomon in his wisdom explained this, saying that the incense is accepted and beloved before the Holy One, blessed be He, more than the burnt-offering slaughtered in the north and more than the peace-offerings slaughtered in the south — that is what he said (Song of Songs 4:16): ״עורי צפון ובואי תימן הפיחי גני יזלו בשמיו יבא דודי לגנו ויאכל פרי מגדיו״ — Awake, O north, and come, O south; breathe upon my garden, let its spices flow; let my beloved come to his garden and eat its precious fruits."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:43.936509620Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}